thanks gentlemen for joining us Steve  first wanted to address something so  couple publications that picked up a  tidbit in my story to both of you about  how you juggle your career at hgtc at  the ESPN I just wanted to give you a  chance to clarify how you do that  well sure Alex I think a lot of people  you leave the game I remember in one of  the things in the article is about Roger  Staubach sent me when you finished  playing run and it really isn't to run  away from the game it's really did not  dwell on it and have a plan and one of  the reasons what the reasons why we sat  down for their article was to talk about  mentorship and how I can get players  today to be thinking about their futures  how to get you know to make sure that  they're prepared with their education  other ways to conspire them so that  because no matter what I played 17 years  and still needed another career and I  built a expertise over the last almost  20 years in private equity but it  certainly I think I think a little  disservice in the article about my  passion and and expertise at ESPN and  for football I mean the truth is I spend  an ordinary out of time on in the fall  preparing for my job I the ESPN  gratefully put a camera into my office  so for an hour and a half on Wednesdays  I talked to PTI and go around the league  and all the issues in SportsCenter and  Thursday morning early morning we have  our production meeting for the Monday  show with our team of Trent and Charles  and and and Randy and and Suzy and then  Monday obviously on the field  pre-production meetings for and so  there's that for me and then on during  the game I mean if there's any  interaction with anybody away from  football it's at the you know we have a  two hour show before the game breaks in  there where we can say hi to people and  on the field and then during the game  the first quarter we spend watching the  game from the field get a feel for it  second and third quarters obviously the  bus are in a suite and then the fourth  quarter back on the field so I think  more than anything there's a little bit  of insecurity I think for players who  leave the game and and want to do  something else they wanted to create a  new profession a new expertise and I've  done that over again for over 20 years  but then my other expertise I don't want  to do disservice and my family to SPN  who you know Alex be honest with you  I've been with ESPN longer I was with  the 49ers and so there's so many rich  relationships there  and so you know and I feel like I've  been doing for 15 years in fact I think  it's probably for me maybe my best year  this year and including the post game of  Super Bowl so I think more than anything  an article certainly don't misconstrue  my passion and and focus for for doing a  great job I feel like there's not in  conflict the two jobs and at all and and  I'm really lucky to have them both so I  think I think anyone who's listened to  you knows certainly you know how  excellent you are at football  commentating and and I will say that the  point of bringing that out in the story  is to simply alert people to the fact  that you do have two things going on at  once which I think a lot of people don't  realize and that's sort of getting into  your hgg see so it's a good segue  tell us how you learned about the  private equity industry and you  mentioned you've been doing this  actually longer and you've been playing  professional football yeah so luckily I  made good friends with Warren Hellman  and Larry Smith seen he was on a board a  PowerBar back when I was playing and I  got introduced to the business world and  then rich Lawson on my left here was the  CEO of a business that I started while I  was finishing playing and so that's how  we got connected and then Bob Gay a good  friend of mine who was with Bain Capital  for years when I was an investor in  those funds gave me an opportunity to  transition you know from football into  private equity and Direct Investing in  2001 with Sorenson capital rich and I  were founding member members there and  so that's how I kind of made my  transition a lot of others from Silicon  Valley relationships that I'd built  while we were playing Harris Barton  Brent Jones ronnie Lott a lot of us were  sit around the locker room and we're  thinking about how can we get involved  this explosion of Silicon Valley around  our Santa Clara practice facility how  can we you know take advantage and learn  and we got great relationships in the  venture capital world and that's kind of  how there's a natural it seems odd when  people meet me and say how are the  worlds you get started in private equity  but there was a natural way being in  Santa Clara night thinking around  Silicon Valley during the you know the  late 90s rich tell us a little bit about  hgg C's strategy because you guys have  an investing philosophy that is pretty  unusual these days in private equity  well it is Alexson it only in this  global economy right in that 17 short  years that Steve had mentioned we've  been able to grow hgtc to a little over  four billion dollars of capital under  management we have over 46,000 employees  involved  our portfolio companies and we continue  to do what we call middle market private  equity control deals there's a  tremendous opportunity today to go back  to basics and what private equity used  to be in the 90s in terms of the average  size and we've we've had a great run  it's been a been a tremendous  opportunity and it's been a great time  to be able to work with Steve along the  way over nearly two decades Alex you  read more about HDC in this week's  business week as I mentioned Steve have  you ever miss if I didn't ask you a  football question or two you did publish  a book recently your autobiography my  life behind the spiral and the first  quote on the back of this book is from  Tom Brady so I have to ask you is Tom  Grady the greatest quarterback of all  time now sure feels like it with five  Super Bowls I mean it's amazing I was  sitting on the field and as I mentioned  I did post game of the Super Bowl and  and we were watching it that last couple  of drives from the side of the from the  field and you think yourself the  enormity of the moment that come back  the greatest comeback in the history of  the Super Bowl and then also what tom  has done over and over again and in the  postgame I referred to him as  relentlessly excellent and and that  really came through as he finished the  game it almost felt inevitable when he  got those opportunities towards the end  of the game and I don't know that  anyone's been able to do that over five  Super Bowls and so obviously with Joe  Montana with all the great players I'm  not great at picking the greatest of all  time because I think each generation  builds on the next is informed by the  previous and so for me who was the  greatest of their generation and Tom  Brady certainly is

